  "Number": 12,
  "Question": "Points \\((\\sqrt{\\pi}, a)\\) and \\((\\sqrt{\\pi}, b)\\) are distinct points on the graph of \\[y^2 + x^4 = 2x^2 y + 1.\\] What is \\(|a-b|?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(1\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\)",
    "C": "\\(2\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\sqrt{1+\\pi}\\)",
    "E": "\\(1 + \\sqrt{\\pi}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Moving the mixed term to the left reveals a perfect square:\n\\[\ny^2-2x^2y+x^4=1,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\n(y-x^2)^2=1.\n\\]\nAt the given horizontal coordinate \\(x=\\sqrt{\\pi}\\), we have \\(x^2=\\pi\\). Hence\n\\[\n(y-\\pi)^2=1.\n\\]\nA real number has square \\(1\\) exactly when it is \\(1\\) or \\(-1\\), so the two possible vertical coordinates are \\(\\pi+1\\) and \\(\\pi-1\\). Because the given points are distinct, \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) must be these two values in some order. Therefore\n\\[\n|a-b|=|(\\pi+1)-(\\pi-1)|=\\boxed{2},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\text{C}\\).",
